text:
{
  "design": {
    "theme": "studio",
    "picture_style": "photo"
  },
  "flow": [
    {"slide":1,"does":"Recall evidence that a reaction is happening","look_at":{"kind":"picture","shows":"Bubbles around magnesium in acid"},"teaches":[]},
    {"slide":2,"does":"Introduce the approved objectives","look_at":{"kind":"none","shows":null},"teaches":[]},
    {"slide":3,"does":"Introduce gas collection and predict a measurable change","look_at":{"kind":"diagram","shows":"A sealed flask connected to a gas syringe"},"teaches":[2]},
    {"slide":4,"does":"Model calculating a mean rate","look_at":{"kind":"diagram","shows":"Gas volume readings at two times"},"teaches":[1]},
    {"slide":5,"does":"Try a near-identical mean-rate calculation","look_at":{"kind":"none","shows":null},"teaches":[1]},
    {"slide":6,"does":"Explain concentration using collision theory","look_at":{"kind":"diagram","shows":"More acid particles in the same volume near magnesium"},"teaches":[3]},
    {"slide":7,"does":"Correct the belief that concentration makes particles faster","look_at":{"kind":"diagram","shows":"Different particle numbers but the same typical speed at equal temperature"},"teaches":[3]},
    {"slide":8,"does":"Check the explanation with a hinge question","look_at":{"kind":"none","shows":null},"teaches":[3]},
    {"slide":9,"does":"Teach a safe, controlled investigation","look_at":{"kind":"diagram","shows":"Gas collection apparatus and controlled conditions"},"teaches":[2]},
    {"slide":10,"does":"Plan and evaluate a concentration investigation","look_at":{"kind":"none","shows":null},"teaches":[2]},
    {"slide":11,"does":"Interpret unfamiliar gas-volume curves","look_at":{"kind":"diagram","shows":"Two reactions reaching the same final gas volume at different times"},"teaches":[1,3]},
    {"slide":12,"does":"Apply the ideas to a flawed investigation","look_at":{"kind":"none","shows":null},"teaches":[2,3]}
  ],
  "title": {
    "template": "title",
    "heading": "Rates of reaction",
    "lead": "Recall: what visible sign suggests that a gas is being made?",
    "picture": {
      "shows": "magnesium ribbon reacting in dilute acid in a laboratory flask",
      "must_see": ["silver magnesium ribbon","bubbles around ribbon","clear liquid"],
      "subject": "generic"
    }
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Measure the gas",
      "lead": "Magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen",
      "points": [
        "Hydrogen pushes the gas-syringe plunger out.",
        "Predict what happens to the reading as the reaction continues."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "labelled-diagram",
        "shows": "An airtight conical flask containing magnesium and hydrochloric acid, connected by a bung and delivery tube to a horizontally clamped graduated gas syringe. The plunger can move freely.",
        "labels": ["Gas syringe (cm³)","Delivery tube","Airtight bung","Hydrochloric acid","Magnesium"]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "equation-hero",
      "heading": "Mean rate: a worked example",
      "lead": "Mean rate is the average over a chosen time interval.",
      "formula": "mean rate = gas volume ÷ time",
      "points": [
        "Volume produced = 36 − 0 = 36 cm³",
        "Time taken = 30 − 0 = 30 s",
        "Mean rate = 36 ÷ 30 = 1.2 cm³/s"
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "The initial and final gas readings for a 30-second interval",
        "alt": "At zero seconds the gas volume is zero cubic centimetres; at 30 seconds it is 36 cubic centimetres.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["Time (s)","Gas (cm³)"],
        "rows": [["0","0"],["30","36"]]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Calculate a mean rate",
      "questions": [
        "A reaction produces 0 cm³ at 0 s and 48 cm³ at 40 s. Calculate its mean rate.",
        "A second reaction produces 0 cm³ at 0 s and 48 cm³ at 60 s. Calculate its mean rate.",
        "Which reaction is faster on average? Use your results."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write the calculation and the unit.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Why does concentration matter?",
      "lead": "A higher concentration means more acid particles in the same volume.",
      "points": [
        "Acid particles collide with the magnesium surface more often.",
        "More collisions succeed each second, so the rate increases."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "particles",
        "shows": "Two equal-sized panels with identical magnesium surfaces and equal liquid volumes. The dilute panel contains eight acid particles; the concentrated panel contains sixteen. Movement arrows have the same range of lengths in both panels. Both panels are at the same temperature.",
        "labels": ["Lower concentration","Higher concentration","Magnesium surface","Same temperature"]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "More particles, not faster particles",
      "lead": "Doubling concentration at the same temperature does not make particles move faster.",
      "points": [
        "Successful collisions need enough energy to react.",
        "More particles mean more collisions each second, not more energy per collision."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "particles",
        "shows": "Two equal-volume acid samples at 20°C beside identical magnesium surfaces. One has twice as many acid particles as the other. Both show the same distribution of movement-arrow lengths, demonstrating unchanged typical speed.",
        "labels": ["Dilute acid: 20°C","Concentrated acid: 20°C","Equal typical speed"]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "hinge",
      "heading": "Choose the best explanation",
      "stem": "Why does more concentrated acid react faster with magnesium at the same temperature?",
      "options": [
        "Acid particles move faster.",
        "Successful collisions happen more often.",
        "Each collision has more energy.",
        "The magnesium gains a larger surface."
      ],
      "correct": 2
    },
    {
      "template": "steps",
      "heading": "Investigate concentration",
      "points": [
        "Use 25 cm³ of acid; change its concentration.",
        "Keep magnesium mass, surface area and temperature the same.",
        "Add magnesium, seal promptly and start timing.",
        "Record gas every 10 s; repeat each test three times."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "labelled-diagram",
        "shows": "A conical flask attached through an airtight bung and delivery tube to a freely moving, clamped gas syringe. Magnesium ribbon of fixed mass and dimensions sits in a fixed volume of hydrochloric acid. A thermometer beside the flask represents checking the initial temperature.",
        "labels": ["Gas syringe (cm³)","Airtight bung","25 cm³ acid","Fixed magnesium mass","Check temperature"]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Plan a fair test",
      "questions": [
        "Test acid at 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 mol/dm³. State what you change and what you measure.",
        "Write a method using a flask, gas syringe and timer. Include readings and repeats.",
        "Explain how you control magnesium mass, surface area and temperature."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write a plan that another pupil could follow.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Interpret the results",
      "lead": "Calculate each mean rate from 0–20 s. Which used stronger acid? Explain why both finish at 40 cm³.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "Two gas-volume curves for identical magnesium masses and surface areas at the same temperature, with acid in excess in both tests",
        "alt": "At 20 seconds A has produced 32 cubic centimetres and B has produced 20. Both finish at 40 cubic centimetres. Magnesium mass and temperature are equal; acid is in excess.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":80,"step":10},
        "y": {"label":"Gas volume (cm³)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": false,
        "series": [
          {"label":"A","points":[[0,0],[10,20],[20,32],[30,38],[40,40],[60,40],[80,40]],"style":"line","axis":"left"},
          {"label":"B","points":[[0,0],[10,11],[20,20],[30,28],[40,34],[60,40],[80,40]],"style":"line","axis":"left"}
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": [
          {"x":44,"y":15,"label":"Equal Mg mass"},
          {"x":44,"y":10,"label":"Equal temperature"},
          {"x":44,"y":5,"label":"Acid in excess"}
        ],
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Can we trust this conclusion?",
      "questions": [
        "Test X: 0.5 mol/dm³ acid, 0.04 g magnesium, 20°C. Test Y: 1.0 mol/dm³ acid, 0.08 g magnesium, 30°C. Y reacts faster. Can concentration alone explain this?",
        "Redesign these tests to isolate the effect of concentration.",
        "Stretch: with mass, surface area and temperature fixed, explain why higher concentration increases the number of successful collisions each second."
      ],
      "instruction": "Evaluate the claim and improve the test.",
      "picture": null
    }
  ]
}
